sched/fair: do not scale the base slice with the number of CPUs

The fair scheduler multiplies its 0.7 ms base slice by 1 + log2 of the
CPU count, capped at 8 CPUs: 2.8 ms on any laptop or desktop of the
last years. A task that wakes up behind a CPU-bound one (the
compositor, the audio thread or the mouse while a build or a game
loads) can wait that long. Keep the slice at 0.7 ms.

The value can still be changed at run time in
/sys/kernel/debug/sched/tunable_scaling and base_slice_ns.

--- a/kernel/sched/fair.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/fair.c
@@ -67,14 +67,16 @@
  *   SCHED_TUNABLESCALING_LOG - scaled logarithmically, *1+ilog(ncpus)
  *   SCHED_TUNABLESCALING_LINEAR - scaled linear, *ncpus
  *
- * (default SCHED_TUNABLESCALING_LOG = *(1+ilog(ncpus))
+ * Raye keeps the base slice at 0.70 msec whatever the number of CPUs:
+ * scaled, an 8-thread laptop gets 2.8 msec, and a task that wakes up
+ * behind a busy one (the compositor behind a build) waits that long.
  */
-unsigned int sysctl_sched_tunable_scaling = SCHED_TUNABLESCALING_LOG;
+unsigned int sysctl_sched_tunable_scaling = SCHED_TUNABLESCALING_NONE;
 
 /*
  * Minimal preemption granularity for CPU-bound tasks:
  *
- * (default: 0.70 msec * (1 + ilog(ncpus)), units: nanoseconds)
+ * (default: 0.70 msec, units: nanoseconds)
  */
 unsigned int sysctl_sched_base_slice			= 700000ULL;
 static unsigned int normalized_sysctl_sched_base_slice	= 700000ULL;
